Task 1: Setting Up Jenkins and the API Environment
Subtask 1.1: Install Java, Jenkins, and Supporting Tools
Install Java, since Jenkins requires it to run:

# Update the system packages
sudo apt update

# Install OpenJDK 11
sudo apt install -y openjdk-11-jdk

# Verify Java installation
java -version
Install Jenkins:

# Add Jenkins repository key
wget -q -O - https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable/jenkins.io.key | sudo apt-key add -

# Add Jenkins repository
sudo sh -c 'echo deb https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable binary/ > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list'

# Update package list
sudo apt update

# Install Jenkins
sudo apt install -y jenkins

# Start Jenkins service
sudo systemctl start jenkins

# Enable Jenkins to start on boot
sudo systemctl enable jenkins

# Check Jenkins status
sudo systemctl status jenkins
Install curl and jq for API interactions:

sudo apt install -y curl jq

curl --version
jq --version
Complete the initial setup by retrieving the admin password:

sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword
Open a web browser and navigate to http://localhost:8080. Use the password above to unlock Jenkins, then:

Select Install suggested plugins
Create an admin user:
Username: admin
Password: admin123
Full name: Jenkins Admin
Email: admin@localhost
Keep the default Jenkins URL: http://localhost:8080/
Click Save and Finish
Deliverable: sudo systemctl status jenkins shows active (running), and http://localhost:8080 loads the Jenkins dashboard after login.

Subtask 1.2: Configure API Authentication
Create an API token:

Go to http://localhost:8080/user/admin/configure
Scroll to the API Token section
Click Add new Token, name it api-automation
Click Generate and copy the token value
Set up environment variables used throughout this lab:

export JENKINS_URL="http://localhost:8080"
export JENKINS_USER="admin"
export JENKINS_TOKEN="your-generated-token-here"

# Test authenticated API call
curl -s -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN $JENKINS_URL/api/json | jq '.version'
Deliverable: The command above prints a version string, e.g. "2.401.3", confirming authenticated API access. Save these three export lines in ~/.jenkins_env and source ~/.jenkins_env at the start of every new terminal session for the rest of this lab.

Task 2: Creating, Triggering, and Verifying Jobs via REST API
Subtask 2.1: Create Freestyle and Parameterized Jobs
Create a simple freestyle job:

cat > simple-job-config.xml << 'EOF'
<?xml version='1.1' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<project>
  <description>Simple job created via REST API</description>
  <keepDependencies>false</keepDependencies>
  <properties/>
  <scm class="hudson.scm.NullSCM"/>
  <canRoam>true</canRoam>
  <disabled>false</disabled>
  <blockBuildWhenDownstreamBuilding>false</blockBuildWhenDownstreamBuilding>
  <blockBuildWhenUpstreamBuilding>false</blockBuildWhenUpstreamBuilding>
  <triggers/>
  <concurrentBuild>false</concurrentBuild>
  <builders>
    <hudson.tasks.Shell>
      <command>echo "Hello from Jenkins API!"
echo "Build number: $BUILD_NUMBER"
echo "Job name: $JOB_NAME"
date</command>
    </hudson.tasks.Shell>
  </builders>
  <publishers/>
  <buildWrappers/>
</project>
EOF

curl -s -X POST \
  -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
  -H "Content-Type: application/xml" \
  --data-binary @simple-job-config.xml \
  "$JENKINS_URL/createItem?name=simple-api-job"

curl -s -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
  "$JENKINS_URL/job/simple-api-job/api/json" | jq '.name'
Create a parameterized job:

cat > param-job-config.xml << 'EOF'
<?xml version='1.1' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<project>
  <description>Parameterized job created via REST API</description>
  <keepDependencies>false</keepDependencies>
  <properties>
    <hudson.model.ParametersDefinitionProperty>
      <parameterDefinitions>
        <hudson.model.StringParameterDefinition>
          <name>MESSAGE</name>
          <description>Message to display</description>
          <defaultValue>Hello World</defaultValue>
          <trim>false</trim>
        </hudson.model.StringParameterDefinition>
        <hudson.model.ChoiceParameterDefinition>
          <name>ENVIRONMENT</name>
          <description>Target environment</description>
          <choices>
            <string>development</string>
            <string>staging</string>
            <string>production</string>
          </choices>
        </hudson.model.ChoiceParameterDefinition>
      </parameterDefinitions>
    </hudson.model.ParametersDefinitionProperty>
  </properties>
  <scm class="hudson.scm.NullSCM"/>
  <canRoam>true</canRoam>
  <disabled>false</disabled>
  <blockBuildWhenDownstreamBuilding>false</blockBuildWhenDownstreamBuilding>
  <blockBuildWhenUpstreamBuilding>false</blockBuildWhenUpstreamBuilding>
  <triggers/>
  <concurrentBuild>false</concurrentBuild>
  <builders>
    <hudson.tasks.Shell>
      <command>echo "Message: $MESSAGE"
echo "Environment: $ENVIRONMENT"
echo "Build started at: $(date)"
sleep 5
echo "Build completed at: $(date)"</command>
    </hudson.tasks.Shell>
  </builders>
  <publishers/>
  <buildWrappers/>
</project>
EOF

curl -s -X POST \
  -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
  -H "Content-Type: application/xml" \
  --data-binary @param-job-config.xml \
  "$JENKINS_URL/createItem?name=param-api-job"

echo "Parameterized job created successfully"
List all jobs:

cat > list-jobs.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== Jenkins Jobs List ==="
curl -s -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
  "$JENKINS_URL/api/json?tree=jobs[name,url,color]" | \
  jq -r '.jobs[] | "\(.name) - Status: \(.color) - URL: \(.url)"'
EOF

chmod +x list-jobs.sh
./list-jobs.sh
Deliverable: list-jobs.sh prints two lines listing simple-api-job and param-api-job with their status color and URL.

Subtask 2.2: Trigger Builds and Monitor Progress
Trigger the simple job:

curl -s -X POST \
  -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
  "$JENKINS_URL/job/simple-api-job/build"

echo "Build triggered for simple-api-job"

sleep 5

curl -s -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
  "$JENKINS_URL/job/simple-api-job/lastBuild/api/json" | \
  jq '{number: .number, result: .result, building: .building}'
Trigger the parameterized job with specific parameter values:

curl -s -X POST \
  -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
  -d "MESSAGE=API Automation Test" \
  -d "ENVIRONMENT=staging" \
  "$JENKINS_URL/job/param-api-job/buildWithParameters"

echo "Parameterized build triggered"

curl -s -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
  "$JENKINS_URL/queue/api/json" | \
  jq '.items[] | {task: .task.name, why: .why}'
Create a reusable build monitoring script:

cat > monitor-build.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

JOB_NAME=$1
if [ -z "$JOB_NAME" ]; then
    echo "Usage: $0 <job-name>"
    exit 1
fi

echo "Monitoring job: $JOB_NAME"

while true; do
    BUILD_INFO=$(curl -s -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
        "$JENKINS_URL/job/$JOB_NAME/lastBuild/api/json")

    BUILD_NUMBER=$(echo $BUILD_INFO | jq -r '.number')
    IS_BUILDING=$(echo $BUILD_INFO | jq -r '.building')
    RESULT=$(echo $BUILD_INFO | jq -r '.result')

    echo "Build #$BUILD_NUMBER - Building: $IS_BUILDING - Result: $RESULT"

    if [ "$IS_BUILDING" = "false" ]; then
        echo "Build completed with result: $RESULT"
        break
    fi

    sleep 5
done
EOF

chmod +x monitor-build.sh
./monitor-build.sh simple-api-job
Deliverable: monitor-build.sh simple-api-job polls until the build finishes and prints Build completed with result: SUCCESS.

Task 3: Updating, Validating, and Testing Jobs via REST API
Subtask 3.1: Update Job Configuration and Verify the Change
Retrieve the current configuration and save a backup:

curl -s -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
    "$JENKINS_URL/job/simple-api-job/config.xml" > current-config.xml
Create an updated configuration:

cat > updated-job-config.xml << 'EOF'
<?xml version='1.1' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<project>
  <description>Updated job via REST API - Now with more features!</description>
  <keepDependencies>false</keepDependencies>
  <properties/>
  <scm class="hudson.scm.NullSCM"/>
  <canRoam>true</canRoam>
  <disabled>false</disabled>
  <blockBuildWhenDownstreamBuilding>false</blockBuildWhenDownstreamBuilding>
  <blockBuildWhenUpstreamBuilding>false</blockBuildWhenUpstreamBuilding>
  <triggers/>
  <concurrentBuild>false</concurrentBuild>
  <builders>
    <hudson.tasks.Shell>
      <command>echo "=== Updated Job Execution ==="
echo "Hello from updated Jenkins API job!"
echo "Build number: $BUILD_NUMBER"
echo "Job name: $JOB_NAME"
echo "Workspace: $WORKSPACE"
echo "Build URL: $BUILD_URL"
echo "Current date: $(date)"
echo "System info: $(uname -a)"
echo "=== End of Execution ==="</command>
    </hudson.tasks.Shell>
  </builders>
  <publishers/>
  <buildWrappers/>
</project>
EOF
Push the update, then verify it took effect by diffing the live configuration against the file you submitted:

curl -s -X POST \
    -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
    -H "Content-Type: application/xml" \
    --data-binary @updated-job-config.xml \
    "$JENKINS_URL/job/simple-api-job/config.xml"

echo "Job configuration submitted"

# Verify the update was applied correctly
curl -s -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
    "$JENKINS_URL/job/simple-api-job/config.xml" | \
    diff -u updated-job-config.xml - && echo "Update verified"
If the XML you submit is malformed (for example, an unclosed tag or invalid element), Jenkins responds with an HTTP 500 error and a stack trace instead of applying the change. Handle this case explicitly:

# Attempt an update with intentionally malformed XML to observe error handling
cat > broken-config.xml << 'EOF'
<?xml version='1.1' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<project>
  <description>Broken config missing closing tag
  <builders>
    <hudson.tasks.Shell>
      <command>echo "this will not apply"</command>
    </hudson.tasks.Shell>
  </builders>
</project>
EOF

HTTP_STATUS=$(curl -s -o response-body.txt -w "%{http_code}" -X POST \
    -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
    -H "Content-Type: application/xml" \
    --data-binary @broken-config.xml \
    "$JENKINS_URL/job/simple-api-job/config.xml")

if [ "$HTTP_STATUS" = "200" ]; then
    echo "Update applied successfully"
else
    echo "Update FAILED with HTTP status $HTTP_STATUS"
    echo "Server response saved in response-body.txt for review"
    echo "Restoring last known good configuration..."
    curl -s -X POST \
        -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
        -H "Content-Type: application/xml" \
        --data-binary @updated-job-config.xml \
        "$JENKINS_URL/job/simple-api-job/config.xml"
    echo "Restore submitted"
fi
Deliverable: The first diff command prints Update verified with no diff output, confirming the live job configuration matches updated-job-config.xml exactly. The malformed-XML test prints Update FAILED with HTTP status 500 (or similar non-200 code) followed by a successful restore of the good configuration.

Subtask 3.2: Build a Final Automated Test Suite
Create a job deletion helper used for cleanup within the test suite:

cat > delete-job.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

JOB_NAME=$1

if [ -z "$JOB_NAME" ]; then
    echo "Usage: $0 <job-name>"
    exit 1
fi

curl -s -X POST \
    -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
    "$JENKINS_URL/job/$JOB_NAME/doDelete" > /dev/null

echo "Job '$JOB_NAME' deleted"
EOF

chmod +x delete-job.sh
Now build the complete, self-contained final test suite:

cat > final-test.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== Jenkins API Automation - Final Test Suite ==="

TESTS_PASSED=0
TESTS_FAILED=0

# Test function: runs a command and records pass/fail
run_test() {
    local test_name="$1"
    local test_command="$2"

    echo -n "Testing $test_name... "

    if eval "$test_command" > /dev/null 2>&1; then
        echo "PASSED"
        TESTS_PASSED=$((TESTS_PASSED + 1))
    else
        echo "FAILED"
        TESTS_FAILED=$((TESTS_FAILED + 1))
    fi
}

# Run tests covering the full API automation workflow
run_test "Jenkins connectivity" \
    "curl -s --connect-timeout 5 '$JENKINS_URL' > /dev/null"

run_test "API authentication" \
    "curl -s -u '$JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN' '$JENKINS_URL/api/json' | jq -e '.version' > /dev/null"

run_test "Job listing" \
    "curl -s -u '$JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN' '$JENKINS_URL/api/json' | jq -e '.jobs' > /dev/null"

run_test "Job creation" \
    "curl -s -X POST -u '$JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN' -H 'Content-Type: application/xml' --data '<project><builders><hudson.tasks.Shell><command>echo test</command></hudson.tasks.Shell></builders></project>' '$JENKINS_URL/createItem?name=final-test-job'"

run_test "Build triggering" \
    "curl -s -X POST -u '$JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN' '$JENKINS_URL/job/final-test-job/build'"

# Give Jenkins a moment to register and run the triggered build
sleep 5

run_test "Build monitoring" \
    "curl -s -u '$JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN' '$JENKINS_URL/job/final-test-job/lastBuild/api/json' | jq -e '.number' > /dev/null"

run_test "Config retrieval" \
    "curl -s -u '$JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN' '$JENKINS_URL/job/final-test-job/config.xml' | grep -q '<project>'"

# Clean up test job regardless of test outcomes
curl -s -X POST -u $JENKINS_USER:$JENKINS_TOKEN \
    "$JENKINS_URL/job/final-test-job/doDelete" > /dev/null 2>&1

echo ""
echo "=== Test Results ==="
echo "Tests Passed: $TESTS_PASSED"
echo "Tests Failed: $TESTS_FAILED"
echo "Total Tests: $((TESTS_PASSED + TESTS_FAILED))"

if [ "$TESTS_FAILED" -eq 0 ]; then
    echo ""
    echo "SUCCESS: All Jenkins API automation tests passed."
    echo "The environment is fully functional for API-driven job management."
    exit 0
else
    echo ""
    echo "FAILURE: $TESTS_FAILED test(s) failed."
    echo "Review the output above, check jenkins-health-check style diagnostics,"
    echo "and re-run ./final-test.sh after resolving the issue."
    exit 1
fi
EOF

chmod +x final-test.sh
./final-test.sh
Deliverable: Running ./final-test.sh prints a PASSED line for each of the seven tests, followed by:

=== Test Results ===
Tests Passed: 7
Tests Failed: 0
Total Tests: 7

SUCCESS: All Jenkins API automation tests passed.
The environment is fully functional for API-driven job management.
The script exits with status code 0 (verify with echo $? immediately after running it). If any test fails, the script exits with status code 1 and prints the FAILURE block instead, naming how many tests failed.

Expected Outcomes
A running Jenkins instance with API token authentication configured, and two working jobs (simple-api-job, param-api-job) created entirely through REST API calls
Working shell scripts (list-jobs.sh, monitor-build.sh, delete-job.sh, final-test.sh) that create, trigger, monitor, update, verify, and clean up Jenkins jobs via the API
A demonstrated understanding of how to detect and recover from a failed configuration update caused by malformed XML
Conclusion
In this lab, you installed and configured Jenkins from scratch, secured API access with token-based authentication, and used curl and jq to create, parameterize, trigger, and monitor jobs entirely through the Jenkins REST API. You then went further than basic CRUD operations by verifying configuration updates with a diff-based check and by deliberately submitting malformed XML to observe and handle a real HTTP 500 failure, restoring the last known good configuration afterward. Finally, you assembled everything into a single automated test suite that exercises the full API workflow, reports pass/fail counts, and exits with a meaningful status code suitable for use in a larger CI/CD pipeline. From here, natural next steps include wiring final-test.sh into a scheduled cron job or an upstream Jenkins pipeline stage, extending the test suite to cover pipeline (Jenkinsfile) jobs via the job/.../doDelete and createItem endpoints, and exploring Jenkins' crumb-based CSRF protection for production environments where API automation must coexist with stricter security settings.
